# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 have "motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual → (self_moves → act_pot_same)"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "motion_is_reduction ∧ reduction_needs_actual → □(self_moves → act_pot_same)"; they differ when only "motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality", "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality", "a thing moves itself, being both mover and moved in the same respect and in the same way" and "the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S3 is read aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "can" does not say which it means
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "motion is nothing else than the reduction of something from potentiality to actuality", "nothing can be reduced from potentiality to actuality except by something in a state of actuality", "a thing moves itself, being both mover and moved in the same respect and in the same way" and "the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect" are true and "necessarily, (if a thing moves itself, being both mover and moved in the same respect and in the same way, then the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect)" is false. Is S3, "Now whatever is in motion is put in motion by another, for nothing can be in motion except it is in potentiality to that towards which it is in motion; whereas a thing moves inasmuch as it is in act.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 have "¬act_pot_same"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "¬◇act_pot_same"; they differ when only "possibly, the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect" is true, which the first allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S7 is read aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 with no modality; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "should" does not say which it means
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered no, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "possibly, the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect" is true and "the same thing is at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect" is false. Is S7, "Now it is not possible that the same thing should be at once in actuality and potentiality in the same respect, but only in different respects.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/readings: S11 is read 2 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 has "moved_by_another → infinite_regress ∨ first_mover"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" is true, which the second allows
aquinas-first-way.md:1: argue/answered: S11: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 and aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" is true and "the series of movers, each put in motion by another, goes on to infinity" and "there is a first mover, put in motion by no other" are false. Is S11, "If that by which it is put in motion be itself put in motion, then this also must needs be put in motion by another, and that by another again.", true then?
aquinas-first-way.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 3 ways: aquinas-first-way.logic.r2 has "¬self_moves → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → subsequent_movers"; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.2 has "some_motion ∧ ¬self_moves → moved_by_another" and "moved_by_another → infinite_regress ∨ first_mover" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → subsequent_movers"; they differ when only "in the world some things are in motion", "a thing moves itself, being both mover and moved in the same respect and in the same way", "whatever is in motion is put in motion by another" and "there are other, subsequent movers that move" are true, which the first allows; aquinas-first-way.logic.r2.3 has "some_motion ∧ ¬self_moves → moved_by_another" and "some_motion ∧ moved_by_another → subsequent_movers" and "infinite_regress ∨ first_mover"
